            <ArticleTitle>Scaphotrapeziotrapezoid arthrodesis: a follow-up study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>397-404</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this study was to review the results of 800 scaphotrapeziotrapezoid fusions performed over a 27-year period.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The patients were evaluated for postoperative range of motion, grip and pinch strength, pain, return to work, arthritis, and complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The overall range of motion was 70% to 80% of the nonoperated side and strength was 69% to 89%. Of the patients 88% returned to previous employment. Arthritis developed in 1.8% of the patients. The overall complication rate was 13.4%.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Scaphotrapeziotrapezoid fusion is a viable treatment option especially for a high-functional-demand young population with significant wrist pathology. Complications, specifically nonunion, can be minimized with careful technique.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
